<p>
  Lizard Island is small, about 6,000 square meters of trees and lawn inside
  Fairchild Tropical Botanic Garden in Miami, a little smaller than a soccer pitch.
  At any given time it holds between 800 and 1,000 anole lizards. Since the fall of
  2015 we have tried to catch every one of them, every spring (May and June) and
  every fall (October and November), with a forced break during COVID.
</p>
<p>
  Each lizard is measured and marked with tiny fluorescent tags, 3 mm long,
  placed in both hind legs, then released. When we
  come back six months later, the lizards we catch again have survived; the ones
  that are gone, mostly, have not. Survival is a simple but robust measure of
  fitness, and tracking it for every individual lets us see which body shapes are
  favored, season by season, in each species.
</p>

<h2>Why an island in a botanic garden?</h2>
<p>
  Long-term studies of natural selection in the wild are rare, and studies that
  follow a whole community of species are rarer still: 163 of 168 published
  studies of selection in the wild have focused on a single species. The most
  famous exception is Peter and Rosemary Grant's 40-year study of Darwin's finches
  on Daphne Major in the Galápagos, and Lizard Island is our attempt at a lizard
  equivalent.
</p>
<p>
  The island's community was originally four species, each specialized for a
  different part of the habitat: the Cuban
  <a href="/miami-lizards/brown-anole/">brown anole</a> near the ground, the
  Hispaniolan <a href="/miami-lizards/bark-anole/">bark anole</a> on tree trunks,
  and two canopy species, the native
  <a href="/miami-lizards/green-anole/">green anole</a> and the Cuban
  <a href="/miami-lizards/knight-anole/">knight anole</a>. Because they share one
  small island, the same season's weather, food and predators act on all of them
  at once.
</p>

<h2>Species that change and stay the same</h2>
<p>
  Many species look much the same for millions of years. The textbook explanation
  is stabilizing selection, which favors the average form and weeds out the
  extremes, yet field studies almost never detect it. Biologists call this the
  &ldquo;paradox of stasis.&rdquo; One of our first major results from Lizard
  Island, published in <em>PNAS</em> in 2023 and based on 1,692 lizards, offers a
  way out. Selection on brown anoles swung from stabilizing in
  the winter of 2015 to directional in the winter of 2016 and back to stabilizing
  in 2017, with quiet periods of little or no selection in between. Added up over
  many seasons, these shifting pressures keep each species near its own peak on a
  shared &ldquo;fitness landscape,&rdquo; with valleys between the peaks that keep
  the species distinct. The species stay put not because selection is constant, but
  because it keeps changing.
</p>

<h2>A newcomer arrives</h2>
<p>
  In late 2017 a fifth species turned up: the Puerto Rican
  <a href="/miami-lizards/crested-anole/">crested anole</a>, an ecological near-twin
  of the brown anole. We had two years of data from before its arrival, which made
  what came next a rare natural experiment. Within four years brown anoles fell from
  75% of adult anoles on the island to 18%, and adult males from 168 to 54, while
  crested anoles rose to 66%. Selection on brown anoles shifted to favor relatively
  longer hind limbs, matching the difference we see between brown anoles that live
  with and without crested anoles across Miami. The result, published in
  <em>Nature Communications</em> in 2024, is direct evidence of natural selection
  driving character displacement in the wild.
</p>

<h2>Every branch on the island</h2>
<p>
  Anoles are built for the perches they use, so we measure the habitat as
  carefully as the lizards. Annual terrestrial LiDAR scans now map the island's
  structure so completely that we know the position and size of every branch, and
  we are building machine-learning tools to measure lizard X-rays and toepad scans
  automatically. In 2025 the study was recognized with a Maxwell/Hanrahan Award in
  Field Biology, and the next phase, asking how behavior shapes natural selection,
  is funded by a Packard Fellowship in Science and Engineering.
</p>
